12.15 Error Handling
Python raises exceptions when something goes wrong; a try/except block catches them so that your script can report a clear message or recover, the counterpart of R’s tryCatch() (see also Section 17.7 for purrr’s safely()).

try:
    df = pd.read_csv("data/missing.csv")
except FileNotFoundError:
    print("Input file not found; check the path")

# Catch several, and always run cleanup
try:
    value = float("abc")
except (ValueError, TypeError) as err:
    print(f"Could not convert: {err}")
finally:
    print("done")
# Could not convert: could not convert string to float: 'abc'
# done

# Raise your own errors when inputs are invalid (R: stop())
def gc_content_checked(seq):
    if not seq:
        raise ValueError("sequence is empty")
    return gc_content(seq)

Common exceptions you will meet: FileNotFoundError, KeyError (missing dictionary key or column), IndexError (position out of range), TypeError, ValueError, and ZeroDivisionError. Reading the last line of a traceback tells you which one occurred; the lines above it show where.

18.13 Numbered and Aligned Equations
Quarto numbers a display equation when you attach an {#eq-...} label after the closing $$, and you refer to it with @eq-... exactly as for figures and tables (Section 5.9):
$$
E = mc^2
$$ {#eq-einstein}

@eq-einstein relates mass and energy.
\[
E = mc^2
\tag{18.1}\]
Equation 18.1 relates mass and energy.
A multi-step derivation belongs in an aligned environment inside the $$ pair. Each line ends with \\, and the & marks the point that lines up vertically (usually just before the =):
$$
\begin{aligned}
\bar{x} &= \frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n} x_i \\
        &= \frac{x_1 + x_2 + \cdots + x_n}{n}
\end{aligned}
$$ {#eq-mean-aligned}
\[
\begin{aligned}
\bar{x} &= \frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n} x_i \\
        &= \frac{x_1 + x_2 + \cdots + x_n}{n}
\end{aligned}
\tag{18.2}\]
The whole block receives one number (Equation 18.2). Use cases in the same way for a piecewise definition:
$$
f(x) =
\begin{cases}
  \lambda e^{-\lambda x} & x \geq 0 \\
  0                      & x &lt; 0
\end{cases}
$$
