21.7 Translating dplyr to SQL
dbplyr translates dplyr verbs to SQL clauses. Let’s see how common operations map:
Filtering and Selecting

mtcars_db |&gt;
  filter(cyl == 6, mpg &gt; 18) |&gt;
  select(mpg, cyl, hp, wt) |&gt;
  show_query()
#&gt; &lt;SQL&gt;
#&gt; SELECT mpg, cyl, hp, wt
#&gt; FROM mtcars
#&gt; WHERE (cyl = 6.0) AND (mpg &gt; 18.0)

Mutating (Creating New Columns)

mtcars_db |&gt;
  mutate(
    efficiency = mpg / wt,
    hp_per_cyl = hp / cyl
  ) |&gt;
  select(mpg, wt, efficiency, hp_per_cyl) |&gt;
  show_query()
#&gt; &lt;SQL&gt;
#&gt; SELECT mpg, wt, mpg / wt AS efficiency, hp / cyl AS hp_per_cyl
#&gt; FROM mtcars

Grouping and Summarizing

mtcars_db |&gt;
  group_by(cyl) |&gt;
  summarize(
    n = n(),
    avg_mpg = mean(mpg, na.rm = TRUE),
    max_hp = max(hp, na.rm = TRUE)
  ) |&gt;
  show_query()
#&gt; &lt;SQL&gt;
#&gt; SELECT cyl, COUNT(*) AS n, AVG(mpg) AS avg_mpg, MAX(hp) AS max_hp
#&gt; FROM mtcars
#&gt; GROUP BY cyl

Arranging (Sorting)

mtcars_db |&gt;
  arrange(cyl, desc(mpg)) |&gt;
  show_query()
#&gt; &lt;SQL&gt;
#&gt; SELECT mtcars.*
#&gt; FROM mtcars
#&gt; ORDER BY cyl, mpg DESC

21.10 Window Functions
SQL window functions perform calculations across sets of rows related to the current row. dbplyr translates functions like lag(), lead(), and rank() to their SQL equivalents:

mtcars_db |&gt;
  group_by(cyl) |&gt;
  mutate(
    rank_mpg = min_rank(desc(mpg)),
    mpg_vs_avg = mpg - mean(mpg, na.rm = TRUE)
  ) |&gt;
  select(cyl, mpg, rank_mpg, mpg_vs_avg) |&gt;
  show_query()
#&gt; &lt;SQL&gt;
#&gt; SELECT
#&gt;   cyl,
#&gt;   mpg,
#&gt;   CASE
#&gt; WHEN (NOT((mpg IS NULL))) THEN RANK() OVER (PARTITION BY cyl, (CASE WHEN ((mpg IS NULL)) THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) ORDER BY mpg DESC)
#&gt; END AS rank_mpg,
#&gt;   mpg - AVG(mpg) OVER (PARTITION BY cyl) AS mpg_vs_avg
#&gt; FROM mtcars

21.11 Best Practices
1. Filter Early
Push filtering to the database rather than collecting all data and filtering in R:

# Good: Filter in database
mtcars_db |&gt;
  filter(mpg &gt; 25) |&gt;
  collect()

# Bad: Collect everything then filter
mtcars_db |&gt;
  collect() |&gt;
  filter(mpg &gt; 25)

2. Select Only Needed Columns

# Good: Select specific columns
mtcars_db |&gt;
  select(mpg, cyl, hp) |&gt;
  collect()

# Bad: Collect all columns
mtcars_db |&gt;
  collect() |&gt;
  select(mpg, cyl, hp)

3. Aggregate in the Database

# Good: Summarize in database
mtcars_db |&gt;
  group_by(cyl) |&gt;
  summarize(avg_mpg = mean(mpg)) |&gt;
  collect()

# Bad: Collect then summarize
mtcars_db |&gt;
  collect() |&gt;
  group_by(cyl) |&gt;
  summarize(avg_mpg = mean(mpg))

4. Close Connections
Always close your database connection when you’re done:

dbDisconnect(con)

E.12 Control Flow

Conditionals
# if-else
if (condition) {
  # code if TRUE
} else if (other_condition) {
  # code if other TRUE
} else {
  # code if all FALSE
}

# Vectorized if-else
ifelse(condition, value_if_true, value_if_false)

# dplyr case_when
case_when(
  condition1 ~ value1,
  condition2 ~ value2,
  TRUE ~ default_value
)


Loops
# for loop
for (i in 1:10) {
  print(i)
}

# while loop
while (condition) {
  # code
}

# break leaves the loop; next skips to the next iteration
for (i in 1:10) {
  if (i == 3) next
  if (i &gt; 5) break
  print(i)
}

# Apply functions (preferred)
apply(m, 1, sum)         # Over matrix rows (2 = columns)
lapply(list, function)   # Returns list
sapply(list, function)   # Returns vector
vapply(x, f, numeric(1)) # Type-safe sapply
tapply(x, group, mean)   # Apply within groups
map(list, function)      # purrr, returns list
map_dbl(list, function)  # purrr, returns double vector
map_chr(x, toupper)      # purrr, character vector
map2_dbl(x, y, ~ .x + .y)          # Two inputs in parallel
map_dfr(files, read_tsv, .id = "source")   # Row-bind results into one tibble


Writing Functions
cv &lt;- function(x, na.rm = TRUE) {
  sd(x, na.rm = na.rm) / mean(x, na.rm = na.rm) * 100
}
cv(c(4, 9, 12, 5))            # Positional argument
cv(x = c(4, 9, 12, 5))        # Named argument

safe_log &lt;- function(x) {
  if (any(x &lt;= 0)) return(NA)  # Explicit return() for an early exit
  log(x)
}

F.6 Control Flow and Functions
# if / elif / else — blocks are marked by indentation, not braces
if x &gt; 10:
    label = "high"
elif x &gt; 5:
    label = "mid"
else:
    label = "low"

# for and while; break and continue
for i in range(5):
    if i == 3:
        continue
    print(i)

while n &gt; 0:
    n -= 1

# Functions: defaults, docstring, type hints, multiple returns
def gc_content(seq: str, digits: int = 3) -&gt; float:
    """Return the GC fraction of a DNA sequence."""
    seq = seq.upper()
    return round((seq.count("G") + seq.count("C")) / len(seq), digits)

def min_max(x):
    return min(x), max(x)          # returns a tuple
lo, hi = min_max(values)

# Anonymous functions
sorted(genes, key=lambda g: len(g))

# Errors
try:
    df = pd.read_csv(path)
except FileNotFoundError as err:
    print(f"Missing file: {err}")
finally:
    print("done")

if not seq:
    raise ValueError("sequence is empty")

